// @test: typecheck-fail
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: context, DI, negative-contexts, typo
// @contexts: strict
// @expect: error(E605)
// @expected-error: E605 "undefined context"
// @description: A forbidden context names a context; a misspelled one is an error, not a silent no-op.
// @spec: 16-context-system.md Section 1.4 - Negative Contexts

// =============================================================================
// Why the negative side is validated too
// =============================================================================
//
// `@contexts: strict` is REQUIRED for this file to test anything. Every
// file carrying a `@test:` header gets lenient context validation —
// "undefined contexts are silently accepted" — which is right for the
// many specs that write `using [Database]` and expect a harness to
// provide it, and which disables exactly the check this file asserts.
// Without the directive this spec checked cleanly, and the harness
// reported "the expected errors never appeared": a spec defeated by its
// own suite, reading as though the defect were fixed.
//
// Skipping existence-validation on `!X` was free while exclusions were
// inert: an unchecked name forbade nothing, and so did a checked one.
// Once exclusions are enforced the asymmetry acquires a cost — a typo
// turns "forbidden" into "silently permitted", and reads to the author
// as a guarantee. `!Databse` must be an error, exactly as `Databse` is.

context Database {
    fn query(&self, sql: Text) -> Text;
}

fn reads_the_database() using [Database] {
    let _ = Database.query("SELECT 1");
}

// E605: `Databse` is not a context. Without this check the function
// would compile and forbid nothing, while reading as a guarantee.
//
// The body is empty ON PURPOSE. An earlier draft called
// `reads_the_database()` here, which made the file report E613 (the
// caller does not declare `Database`) and stop before reaching the
// typo — a test that would have passed for the wrong reason, on a
// diagnostic about something else. Measured: with the call, E605 does
// not appear at all.
fn typo_in_the_exclusion() using [!Databse] {
}

fn main() {
    typo_in_the_exclusion();
}
